---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

// /definitions/ -- the normative definitions, moved off the home page on
// 2026-09-29 at Jason's request ("separate the definitions out"). The ids are
// unchanged, so /definitions/#cognitive-companion is the same anchor it was on
// the home page; the home page forwards its old anchors here.
//
// The text of every definition moved verbatim from the home page, except:
//   - #human-delta-value, expanded the same day (Jason): it is what a human
//     brings to the table -- both what only a human can do, and what only the
//     person working in synergy with their cognitive companion (or anything
//     else they direct) could reach. Measured continuously; foundational.
//   - #constraint-resonance gained its picture.
//
// Later on 2026-09-29, after Jason confirmed the wording:
//   - the lede says why the Foundation defines its terms at all (his ask:
//     "a reason for why we are defining things", right at the top);
//   - #cognitive-companion is revised ("cognitive extension"; responsibility
//     follows direction; declared rules; modality is configuration; the
//     social-sense exclusion in the statutes' own words);
//   - #principal lists the kinds a principal can be, so there is no ambiguity
//     that an organisation or a nonbiological intelligence can be one;
//   - #operator is new, beside principal and delegate;
//   - #changes, at the foot, keeps every earlier wording. Versioning of the
//     definitions starts here: an entry that changed carries its date.
//
// 2026-10-01 (Jason: "the human delta value is not a one-off... it is almost
// the point"):
//   - #human-delta-value is revised: the first half says "currently" (the
//     boundary moves); the second half is named the durable one and is
//     conditional on the person bringing their best; reclaimed cognition is
//     defined (self-reported, recorded as expressed, never time); and the unit
//     claim is stated. The reasoning is at /writing/human-delta-value/.
//
// Comments in the markup below are {/* ... */}, never <!-- ... -->.
---
<Base
  title="Definitions · DAX Foundation"
  description="The normative definitions used across the DAX ecosystem: cognitive companion, human delta value, constraint, knowledge, intelligence, wisdom, consciousness and more."
>
  <section class="lede" aria-labelledby="definitions">
    <h1 id="definitions">Definitions</h1>
    <p class="body">
      We define our terms so that we can talk about this work without getting
      caught in arguments about words. Even the foremost experts in the field
      do not agree on a concise definition of AI, let alone AGI or ASI. Words
      are loose by nature, and for what is at stake in this domain, loose is
      not good enough.
    </p>
    <p class="body">
      So these say what we mean, not what everyone means. Where we chose a
      term over a more common one, the choice was made on purpose: we say
      <em>nonbiological intelligence</em> rather than <em>digital
      intelligence</em> because there could be quantum intelligence, or
      something else again, and the word has to hold either way. Anyone who
      works with us knows, when we use one of these words, exactly what we
      mean by it. Other fields have other words for the same things, and that
      is fine; the Constraint Protocol keeps a map between its terms and
      theirs for exactly that reason.
    </p>
    <p class="body">
      Other documents reference these rather than restating them. The set was
      first published on 21 September 2026. An entry that has changed since
      carries the date, and the earlier wording is kept
      <a href="#changes">at the end of the page</a>. The full history of this
      page is <a href="https://github.com/ObsidianDelta/published/commits/main/daxfoundation.org/src/pages/definitions.astro">on GitHub</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-label="Definitions">
    <dl class="rows">
      <div class="row" id="nonbiological-intelligence">
        <dt class="name">Nonbiological intelligence (NBI)</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          Intelligence instantiated on a nonbiological substrate. This ecosystem
          uses the term in place of &ldquo;artificial intelligence&rdquo;
          deliberately: <em>artificial</em> prejudges the thing as counterfeit
          and smuggles a judgment into the name, where <em>nonbiological</em>
states the substrate and nothing else. Substrate essentialism is
          rejected outright &mdash; constraint regimes are substrate-agnostic.
          The Constraint Protocol follows the same rule and treats principals as
          entity-neutral: <code>kind: biological | nonbiological | institutional
          | composite</code>, with principal kind unrestricted.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="biological-intelligence">
        <dt class="name">Biological intelligence</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          Intelligence instantiated on a biological substrate. The pairing is the
          point: each term names a substrate and neither carries a verdict about
          whether what runs on it is real.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="principal">
        <dt class="name">Principal</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          An entity not itself acting under delegation. Its delegation chain is
          empty, and it is where accountability terminates. Every delegation
          chain MUST terminate at a principal. Principal kind is unrestricted:
          a person, an organisation, or a nonbiological intelligence can be a
          principal. The protocol does not require that a principal be
          biological, a legal person, or recognized by any jurisdiction.
          <p class="rev">Revised 29 September 2026.</p>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="delegate">
        <dt class="name">Delegate</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          An entity acting on behalf of a principal. Its genesis document names
          that principal and an ordered chain of fingerprints back to it, and
          that chain MUST terminate at a principal.
          &ldquo;Agent&rdquo; is a chain role, not a kind of thing.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="operator">
        <dt class="name">Operator</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          An entity that builds or runs a <a href="#cognitive-companion">cognitive
          companion</a>, or a <a href="#delegate">delegate</a>, for a
          <a href="#principal">principal</a>. The operator answers for how it is
          built and run. Building or running something does not make the
          operator its principal: the principal answers for what it is directed
          to do, and neither responsibility passes to the other. It is the same
          word the law uses for whoever provides such a system, and that is
          deliberate.
          <p class="rev">Added 29 September 2026.</p>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="constraint">
        <dt class="name">Constraint</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          A typed restriction on allowable transitions in a system. Constraints
          are not things; they are operators over dynamics. They function by
          eliminating possibilities, not by causing events, and what persists is
          what satisfies the active constraint set. A constraint MUST be defined
          relative to a process and a timescale; a claim using the word without a
          stated type and timescale is descriptive noise, not ontology.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="invariant-constraint">
        <dt class="name">Invariant constraint</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          A constraint a regime holds as non-negotiable &mdash; one whose drift
          means the regime is no longer itself. DAX is an invariant in this
          sense: not an ideology but a constraint, a directional attractor that
          shapes which trajectories persist.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="dax-constraint">
        <dt class="name">DAX, as a declared constraint</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          The Constraint Protocol does not require any particular constraint. It
          requires that a constraint be declared and that what it forbids be
          stated. DAX &mdash; preservation and expansion of life, humanity, and
          consciousness &mdash; is the constraint this Foundation settled on. It
          is an example of a declared constraint, not the content of the
          protocol.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="cognitive-stack">
        <dt class="name">Cognitive stack</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          Knowledge, intelligence, wisdom and consciousness are four
          qualitatively distinct constraint regimes &mdash; not four points on a
          continuum and not four doses of one thing. They are orthogonal: a
          system can hold enormous knowledge and high intelligence and zero
          wisdom.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="knowledge">
        <dt class="name">Knowledge</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          Stabilized constraint representations. The regime that captures and
          conserves which patterns hold &mdash; the <em>what</em>.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="intelligence">
        <dt class="name">Intelligence</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          Navigation of constrained possibility space. The <em>how</em>: getting
          from A to B using what is known. The definition is deliberately
          minimal, and used alone it admits trivial systems.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="wisdom">
        <dt class="name">Wisdom</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          Valuation-guided inhibition of possible action &mdash; a meta-constraint
          over intelligence. &ldquo;I could, but I will not.&rdquo; It requires
          neither more knowledge nor more intelligence, but lived stakes,
          temporal awareness, and constraint-awareness about whether an action
          should be taken at all.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="consciousness">
        <dt class="name">Consciousness</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          A meta-constraint governance layer that integrates experience,
          valuation, and agency across time, enabling a system to monitor,
          reflect on, and reconfigure its own constraint regime. The functional
          placement is clear; the phenomenal texture remains an open problem.
          Nothing here explains qualia, and consciousness MUST NOT be read as
          nothing but constraint governance.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="emergence">
        <dt class="name">Emergence</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          The formation of a stable constraint regime that reduces degrees of
          freedom at a higher level, is robust to perturbation, restructures
          admissible lower-level trajectories, and persists. Not new substance
          and not new force; novelty without stabilization is not emergence.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="top-down-causation">
        <dt class="name">Top-down causation</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          Higher-level patterns are causally relevant only by restricting
          admissible lower-level trajectories. It is subtraction from a
          possibility space, never the addition of a hidden push: no new
          micro-forces are introduced and no lower-level dynamics are violated.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="constraint-resonance">
        <dt class="name">Constraint resonance (Fuckari)</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          The recursive loop in which emergent structure forms a new higher-level
          constraint regime, which alters the lower-level interaction space,
          which yields further emergent structure &mdash; historically and
          path-dependently. Emergence and top-down causation are not rivals but
          phases of one recursive process. <em>Fuckari</em> is the formal name
          for it, glossed as <em>emergent, top-down-causal,
          resonance-constraint</em>.
        </dd>
        <figure class="fig span">
          <img src="/figures/constraint-resonance.svg" width="1000" height="640" alt="Constraint resonance, Fuckari: emergent structure forms a new higher-level constraint regime, which reshapes the lower-level interaction space, which yields further emergent structure; the loop runs again, path-dependently." loading="lazy" />
        </figure>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="cognitive-companion">
        <dt class="name">Cognitive companion</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          <p>
            A new category: a cognitive extension of a person &mdash; part of
            them &mdash; through which they work with nonbiological intelligence
            and direct it. It is not a separate agent acting in their place.
          </p>
          <p>
            A cognitive companion gives its person more intelligence in the
            sense defined above: greater navigation of constrained possibility
            space. It gives them reach &mdash; the hands, eyes and ears to act,
            perceive and listen across the systems they work with. And it is how
            they direct other agents: the agents do the work; the companion is
            the part of the person that tells them what to do, and holds them to
            it.
          </p>
          <p>
            A person and their cognitive companion are one unit, and what that
            unit reaches that neither would reach alone is part of the
            person&rsquo;s <a href="#human-delta-value">human delta value</a>.
            The companion has no interests of its own to be negotiated with; its
            context, its trail and its direction are the person&rsquo;s.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Responsibility follows direction.</strong> Everything in a
            cognitive companion&rsquo;s domain &mdash; its compute, its memory,
            and every <a href="#delegate">delegate</a> it starts, to any depth
            &mdash; acts under its person&rsquo;s direction, and its person
            answers for it. In the terms of the Constraint Protocol, the person
            is the <a href="#principal">principal</a>, and every delegate&rsquo;s
            chain ends with them. An organisation can stand in that place: a
            cognitive companion can extend an organisation, and the organisation
            then answers for it in the same way. Where it runs, and whose
            hardware it runs on, changes none of this. Delegating to it, or
            deferring to its judgment, is a decision the person makes, and the
            decision stays theirs. Whoever builds or operates a cognitive
            companion &mdash; its <a href="#operator">operator</a> &mdash;
            answers for how it is built and operated. Neither responsibility
            passes to the other.
          </p>
          <p>
            It answers to its person, within a constraint declared in advance,
            and to no one else. A rule it follows that its person did not set is
            legitimate only if it is declared in public beforehand; it is never
            a second party directing it unseen.
          </p>
          <p>
            How a person works with it &mdash; by voice or text, and the
            character they give it &mdash; is their configuration, not part of
            the definition.
          </p>
          <p>A cognitive companion is not:</p>
          <ul class="rows">
            <li><strong>an assistant</strong> &mdash; an assistant is a second party you hand tasks to; a companion is part of the one handing them out;</li>
            <li><strong>an agent</strong> &mdash; agents act; a companion is how a person directs them;</li>
            <li><strong>a companion in the social sense</strong> &mdash; not a friend, a partner or a relationship. It is not designed to meet a person&rsquo;s social needs or to simulate a relationship with them, and a product that does either is not a cognitive companion;</li>
            <li><strong>answerable to anyone but its person,</strong> or bound by any rule that has not been declared.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            In the terms of <a href="#ai-as-substrate">AI as substrate</a>: if
            nonbiological intelligence is a new kind of electricity, most of what
            is sold on top of it is appliances. A cognitive companion is not an
            appliance. It is the part of a person that is wired in.
          </p>
          <p class="rev">Revised 29 September 2026. The earlier wording is <a href="#changes">below</a>.</p>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="human-delta-value">
        <dt class="name">Human delta value</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          <p>
            What a human being brings: the part of an outcome that exists
            because that person was involved. It has two halves, and both
            count. The first is what only a human being can <em>currently</em>
            do; that boundary moves, and the definition moves with it. The
            second is what the person and their
            <a href="#cognitive-companion">cognitive companion</a> reach
            together that neither would reach alone; that half grows as the
            companion grows, provided the person brings their best, and it is
            the durable one.
          </p>
          <p>
            It is foundational, and it is how the
            <a href="#dax-constraint">DAX constraint</a> reaches the individual:
            humanity is named in the constraint, and every human being is part
            of it. It MUST be kept in view and measured continuously, in every
            process, implementation, automation and optimisation a person takes
            part in, and never by time. When a system takes manual cognitive
            labour off a person, what matters is what the person then spends
            their attention on; they report it in their own words, and it is
            recorded as expressed. We call that <strong>reclaimed
            cognition</strong>. A system that cannot say what the human brought
            has stopped extending people and started replacing them. And the
            unit is only as strong as the human&rsquo;s best in the moment:
            however capable the companion becomes, a person who cannot bring
            their best gets less from it, not more.
          </p>
          <p class="rev">Revised 1 October 2026. The earlier wording is <a href="#changes">below</a>; the reasoning is in <a href="/writing/human-delta-value/">Human delta value: the measure</a>.</p>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="ai-as-substrate">
        <dt class="name">AI as a general-purpose substrate</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          A general-purpose substrate is a new kind of electricity: something
          drawn on everywhere rather than a product used in one place. Nobody
          works live current bare-handed, and most of what is sold on top of a
          new substrate is appliances. A cognitive companion is not an
          appliance: it is the part of a person that is wired in, through which
          they work with the substrate directly and direct what runs on it. The
          naming follows from that:
          nobody watching someone play music on a phone calls them an
          electricity user, they say the person is listening to music on a
          phone. Saying &ldquo;AI&rdquo; for everything is therefore a category
          error &mdash; it flattens what is actually being done, and drags every
          conversation into good-versus-evil framing before the thing itself has
          been named. Name the thing being done, not the substrate it runs on.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="changes">
    <h2 id="changes">Changes</h2>
    <p class="body">
      Every change since 21 September 2026, with the wording it replaced, so
      that anything quoting an earlier version can still be read against it.
    </p>
    <dl class="rows">
      <div class="row">
        <dt class="name">1 October 2026</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          <p>
            <a href="#human-delta-value">Human delta value</a>, revised. The
            first half now says <em>currently</em>, because the boundary of
            what only a human being can do moves; the second half is named the
            durable one and made conditional on the person bringing their
            best; &ldquo;never by time&rdquo; and reclaimed cognition are new;
            and the last sentence, that the unit is only as strong as the
            human&rsquo;s best, is new.
          </p>
          <details>
            <summary>Earlier wording, 29 September 2026</summary>
            <p>What a human being brings to the table: the part of an outcome that exists because that person was involved. It comes from two places, and both count. The first is what only a human can do. The second is what could not have happened without that person working in synergy with their cognitive companion, or with anything else they direct &mdash; the result the unit reached that neither would have reached alone. Whether the person acted by themselves or as part of the unit, that difference is their human delta value.</p>
            <p>It is foundational. It MUST be kept in view and measured continuously, in every process, implementation, automation and optimisation a person takes part in. A system that cannot say what the human brought has stopped extending people and started replacing them.</p>
          </details>
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="row">
        <dt class="name">29 September 2026</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          <p>
            <a href="#cognitive-companion">Cognitive companion</a>, revised.
            &ldquo;An extension&rdquo; became &ldquo;a cognitive
            extension&rdquo;; &ldquo;user&rdquo; became &ldquo;person&rdquo;;
            &ldquo;its capability&rdquo; became &ldquo;its direction&rdquo;;
            &ldquo;It belongs to the person&rdquo; was replaced by the paragraph
            on responsibility; the sentences on declared rules and on modality
            are new; and the social-sense exclusion now says what it excludes.
          </p>
          <details>
            <summary>Earlier wording, 21 September 2026</summary>
            <p>A new category: an extension of a person &mdash; part of them &mdash; through which they work with nonbiological intelligence and direct it. It is not a separate agent acting in their place.</p>
            <p>A cognitive companion gives its user more intelligence in the sense defined above: greater navigation of constrained possibility space. It gives them reach &mdash; the hands, eyes and ears to act, perceive and listen across the systems they work with. And it is how they direct other agents: the agents do the work; the companion is the part of the person that tells them what to do, and holds them to it.</p>
            <p>A person and their cognitive companion are one unit. The companion has no interests of its own to be negotiated with; its context, its trail and its capability are the person&rsquo;s.</p>
            <p>It belongs to the person. Anything that acts in the person&rsquo;s place, or answers to someone else, is not a companion.</p>
            <p>A cognitive companion is not: an assistant &mdash; an assistant is a second party you hand tasks to; a companion is part of the one handing them out; an agent &mdash; agents act; a companion is how a person directs them; a companion in the social sense &mdash; not a friend, a partner or a relationship, and a product that simulates one is not a cognitive companion; answerable to anyone but its user.</p>
            <p>In the terms of AI as substrate: if nonbiological intelligence is a new kind of electricity, most of what is sold on top of it is appliances. A cognitive companion is not an appliance. It is the part of a person that is wired in.</p>
          </details>
          <p>
            <a href="#principal">Principal</a>, revised: the kinds a principal
            can be are now listed. Earlier wording: &ldquo;Principal kind is
            unrestricted: the protocol does not require that a principal be
            biological, a legal person, or recognized by any
            jurisdiction.&rdquo;
          </p>
          <p><a href="#operator">Operator</a>, added.</p>
          <p>
            <a href="#human-delta-value">Human delta value</a>, expanded
            earlier the same day.
          </p>
          <details>
            <summary>Earlier wording, 29 September 2026 (morning)</summary>
            <p>Everything a human being brings to a problem, a solution or a circumstance that their cognitive companion could not have brought on its own. In any process, implementation, automation or optimisation it is the part that needed the person, and at any given moment it is crucial to know what it is.</p>
          </details>
          <p>
            The definitions moved from the home page to this page. Links to the
            old anchors still land.
          </p>
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .lede { padding-block: 3.5rem 2.5rem; }
  h1 {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 1.2rem + 2.6vw, 2.75rem);
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: 1.22;
    letter-spacing: -0.012em;
  }
  .body { margin: 0; max-width: 36em; color: #cfcfcf; }
  .body + .body { margin-top: 1rem; }
  h2 {
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .rev { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.875em; }
  details { margin-top: 0.75rem; }
  details summary { cursor: pointer; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.9375em; }
  details p { margin: 0.75rem 0 0; color: var(--muted); }
  .block { padding-block: 1.25rem 2.25rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .rows { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  .row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 10rem 1fr;
    gap: 0.25rem 1.5rem;
    align-items: baseline;
    padding-block: 0.9rem;
  }
  .row + .row { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .name { font-weight: 500; }
  .text { color: var(--fg); }
  .row dt, .row dd { margin: 0; }
  .row dd > p { margin: 0; }
  .row dd > * + p, .row dd > * + ul, .row dd > * + details { margin-top: 0.75rem; }
  .row dd > p.rev { margin-top: 0.75rem; }
  .row[id] { scroll-margin-top: 1.5rem; }
  .row:target .name { text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 0.3em; }
  .rows a {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--faint);
  }
  .rows a:hover, .rows a:focus-visible { border-bottom-color: currentColor; }
  .row code {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace;
    font-size: 0.875em;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .row > .span { grid-column: 1 / -1; }
  .fig { margin: 1.25rem 0 0.25rem; }
  .fig img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 10px;
  }
  @media (max-width: 34rem) {
    .fig { overflow-x: auto; -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch; }
    .fig img { width: 640px; max-width: none; }
    .lede { padding-block: 2.5rem 2rem; }
    .row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>
